    <section class="prose prose-lg mt-16 max-w-3xl">
      <h2>Is soldering safe for teens? Supervision and safety</h2>
      <p>Yes, with sensible habits. The main hazard is heat: according to CircuitMess, soldering involves temperatures above 300°C (570°F).</p><h3>How much supervision</h3><p>There is no official standard. CircuitMess describes the 13+ stage as ready for advanced builds, often with minimal supervision. Our recommendation: let experience set the level as much as age. A first-timer at 16 still benefits from an adult in the room for the first session. Step back once the teen reliably returns the iron to its stand, works with ventilation and unplugs when done. For the detailed version, read our <a href="/guides/best-soldering-kits-for-kids-and-teens">parent's guide to soldering with kids and teens</a>.</p><h3>The bench rules</h3><ul><li><strong>The iron lives in its stand</strong> whenever it is not touching the board.</li><li><strong>Ventilate.</strong> Open a window at minimum. For regular indoor soldering, use a carbon-filter fan like the <a href="/reviews/kotto-solder-fume-extractor">KOTTO</a>. Our <a href="/guides/soldering-fume-safety">soldering fume safety</a> guide explains what is in the smoke.</li><li><strong>Lead-free solder by default.</strong> If leaded solder is ever used, wash hands afterward and keep food and drinks away from the bench.</li><li><strong>Safety glasses</strong> for every session.</li><li><strong>A heat-resistant work surface</strong>, not the bedspread or a varnished table.</li><li><strong>Unplug when finished.</strong></li></ul><p>Before the first session, read our <a href="/guides/how-to-solder-for-beginners">beginner's soldering guide</a> together.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="prose prose-lg mt-16 max-w-3xl">
      <h2>What comes after the first kit</h2>
      <p>Teens who enjoy their first build usually want a harder one. A sensible ladder:</p><ol><li><strong>A through-hole project</strong> with a clear payoff: the console, clock, radio or speaker above.</li><li><strong>A denser board with a sensor and display:</strong> the <a href="/reviews/banria-color-recognition-soldering-kit">BANRIA Color Recognition kit</a>, for finer joints and real electronics concepts.</li><li><strong>Surface-mount practice:</strong> the <a href="/reviews/gikfun-smd-smt-welding-practice-board-ek7028">Gikfun EK7028</a>, working down through smaller component sizes.</li><li><strong>Hot air and rework:</strong> the next skill after hand-soldered SMD, covered in our <a href="/guides/smd-rework-hot-air-and-reflow">SMD rework guide</a>.</li></ol><p>A teen drawn to code can branch off at any point with the <a href="/reviews/amomii-testudo-soldering-practice-kit">amomii Testudo</a>, whose Arduino-compatible microcontroller turns a soldering project into something to program. For more builds beyond this list, browse our <a href="/best-soldering-project-kits">soldering project kits</a>.</p><p>When the iron starts holding them back, such as struggling with fine tips or wanting a better station for a growing bench, see our <a href="/best-soldering-stations">soldering stations</a> and <a href="/best-soldering-irons">soldering irons</a> roundups. The <a href="/electronics-workbench-essentials">workbench essentials</a> guide covers the order to add tools in.</p>
